{"name":"Bungalow White","hex":"#e4d3c8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bungalow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A single-storey house, typically with rooms all on one level, or sometimes also with upper rooms set into the roof space.","A thatched or tiled one-story house in India surrounded by a wide veranda; a similar house in this style."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bungalow"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bungalow","blocks":[{"text":"A bungalow is a small house or cottage that is typically single- or one-and-a-half-storey. If a smaller upper storey exists, then it is frequently set in the roof and windows that come out from the roof. It may be surrounded by wide verandas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/149MyrtleReedsburgWI.JPG/330px-149MyrtleReedsburgWI.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bungalow","wikidata":"Q850107"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Elevation and floor plan of an Australian bungalow-style residence, 1939.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStateLibQld_2_228966_Home_Building_Publishing_Company_home%2C_Design_No._3%2C_1939.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/100_Bungalows_of_Architectural_Distinction_-_DPLA_-_45a4746debb1bb15282e0547198ab930_%28page_56%29_%28cropped